# TypeScript

Strict TypeScript. No `any`, and no non-null assertion to paper over a missing check.

Expected user outcomes are values. Throw only for broken invariants and failed I/O.

```typescript
// Bad: control flow via throw, optional fields that can contradict each other
function start(game: { phase: string; players?: Player[] }): void {
  if (game.players?.length !== 4) throw new Error("nope");
}

// Good: a result, using the shared reason constants
type StartResult =
  | { ok: true; game: ActiveGame }
  | { ok: false; reason: RejectReason };

function start(game: OpenGame, userId: string): StartResult {
  if (game.players.length !== 4) return { ok: false, reason: Reason.needFour };
  // ...
}
```

Extract a function when a second caller needs it. Keep Slack payloads and AWS types out of `src/domain`.
